const RAW_EN=`# Get started with data analytics|What data analysis is and who does it, and a first tour of Power BI.|data-analytics-microsoft
## Discover data analysis|The journey starts with understanding what we do with data and who does the work.
What is data analysis?|Data analysis turns raw numbers and records into clear answers that help you make a decision. The journey usually has four stages: collect the data, clean the errors out of it, analyze it to find patterns, then present the result in a report.^Example: a shop records its sales every day. The analyst collects the records and fixes the errors, then finds that one product sells more in the holidays, so they suggest stocking more of it.|flow:Collect,Clean,Analyze,Present,Decide|Which step comes right after "Clean" in the diagram?~Analyze~Collect~Decide|0
Data roles|Data teams have different roles. A data engineer prepares the sources and builds the pipelines that move data. A data analyst turns ready data into reports and dashboards. A data scientist builds models that predict the future.^Power BI is the main tool of the data analyst. In small teams one person may do more than one role.|flow:Data engineer (prepares),Data analyst (presents),Data scientist (predicts)|Which role usually builds Power BI reports and dashboards?~Data analyst~Data engineer~Data scientist|0
## Start building reports in Power BI|Meet the parts of Power BI and the five steps of any project.
The three parts of Power BI|Power BI is a family of three parts that work together. Power BI Desktop is a free Windows app where you build the report: you connect to data, clean it, and draw visuals. Power BI Service is a cloud website where you publish the report, share it, and refresh its data. The mobile app lets you browse reports anywhere.^Rule of thumb: build in Desktop, then publish and share in the Service.|flow:Power BI Desktop (build),Power BI Service (publish and share),Mobile app (browse)|Where do you usually create the report first?~Power BI Desktop~The mobile app~The Service website|0
Your first report journey|Every Power BI project goes through five stops: connect to the source, clean the data in Power Query, build the model and relationships, draw the report, then publish it. Keeping this order saves you a lot of time.^Example: if you draw the report before cleaning the data, the numbers will be wrong and you will have to redo the work.|flow:Connect,Clean,Model,Report,Publish|Your sales data has errors. At which stop do you fix them before building the model?~Clean~Publish~Report|0
# Prepare data for analysis|Get data from its sources, then clean and transform it in Power Query.|prepare-data-power-bi
## Get data|How to connect to sources and choose the load mode.
Data sources|Get Data is the button where you start. Power BI connects to hundreds of sources: files (Excel and CSV), databases (such as SQL Server), and online services. You pick the source, look at a preview of the data, then choose Load to load it directly or Transform Data to clean it first.^Tip: choose Transform Data if you are not sure the data is clean.|table:Source,Type/Excel,File;SQL Server,Database;Website,Online service|According to the table, which source is a database?~SQL Server~Excel~Website|0
Import or DirectQuery|When you connect to a source you choose how to load it. Import copies the data into the Power BI file, so the report is fast, but the data only updates when you refresh. DirectQuery copies nothing and asks the source every time, so the data is fresher but slower.^Start with Import unless the data is huge or you need live numbers.|table:Mode,Speed,Data freshness/Import,High,As of the last refresh;DirectQuery,Slower,Near real time|According to the table, which mode is usually faster?~Import~DirectQuery~Both are the same|0
## Clean, transform, and load|Prepare data in Power Query before analysis.
Cleaning columns and rows|Before analysis you must clean the data in Power Query. The most common jobs are removing duplicate rows (Remove Duplicates), handling blanks (Replace Values), trimming extra spaces (Trim), and setting each column's type (text, number, date). Every step is recorded and replayed automatically on refresh.^Example: "Ali " with an extra space counts as a different person from "Ali", so we use Trim.|table:Name,City/Ali,Riyadh;Sara,(blank);Ali,Riyadh|What are the two problems visible in the table?~A duplicate row and a blank cell~There are no problems~An extra column|0
Combining tables: Append and Merge|Do you have more than one table? There are two ways to combine them. Append puts the rows of one table under another, and suits tables with the same columns, like each month's sales. Merge adds columns from another table through a shared column, like adding the city name to a customers table.^Remember: Append = more rows, Merge = more columns.|flow:January table + February table,Append,One table with more rows|To put January sales under February sales you use?~Append~Merge~Pivot|0
# Model data in Power BI|Design the model and relationships, write calculations in DAX, and speed up performance.|model-power-bi
## Design the model|The star schema and relationships.
Fact and dimension tables|The best design for a Power BI model is the star schema. In the middle is the fact table, which holds events and numbers such as sales. Around it are dimension tables that describe the facts: product, customer, date, store.^This design makes calculations simpler and reports faster, and avoids repetition.|star:Sales/Product,Customer,Date,Store|What sits in the center of the diagram?~The fact table (Sales)~The Date table~The Customer table|0
Relationships between tables|A relationship connects two tables through a shared column. The most common type is one-to-many (1:*): each customer appears once in the Customers table, but may appear many times in the Orders table. Filters flow from the "one" side to the "many" side.^Example: when you select one customer, Power BI shows only that customer's orders.|star:Orders/Customers|In the Customers to Orders relationship, which side is the "one" side?~Customers~Orders~Both|0
## Add calculations with DAX|Calculated columns and measures.
Calculated column or measure|DAX is the formula language of Power BI. A calculated column is computed once when the data refreshes and stored in every row. A measure is computed at the moment of display, based on the filters you choose, so it is the right choice for totals and ratios.^Example: total sales must change when you pick a city, so we write it as a measure.|table:Type,When it is computed,Responds to filters?/Calculated column,At refresh,No;Measure,At display time,Yes|Which one responds to filters?~The measure~The calculated column~Neither|0
The CALCULATE function|CALCULATE is the most important DAX function. It computes a value after changing the filter. For example, CALCULATE(SUM(Sales[Amount]), Sales[City] = "Riyadh") gives you Riyadh sales whatever filter is selected.^Use it when you want a number for a specific city, year, or product next to the total.|bar:All,Riyadh,Jeddah/300,120,90|According to the chart, what are Riyadh sales?~120~300~90|0
## Improve performance|Shrink the model and measure slow visuals.
Model size|The bigger the model, the slower the report. The easiest fixes: delete columns you do not use, reduce columns with many unique values (like time down to the second), and prefer Import with trimmed data.^Example: a table has 50 columns but you use only 15, so delete the rest.|bar:50 columns,15 columns/80,25|The chart shows model size in megabytes. Which model is lighter?~The one with 15 columns~The one with 50 columns~They are equal|0
Performance testing|Performance Analyzer is a Desktop tool that measures the load time of each visual in milliseconds and separates query time from drawing time. Open it from the View tab, choose Performance analyzer, and press Start recording.^Start by improving the slowest visual first.|bar:Sales chart,Map,Table/120,950,200|The numbers are in milliseconds. Which visual is slowest?~The map~The table~The sales chart|0
# Design visuals and reports|Pick the right chart and build a clear, interactive report.|visualize-data-power-bi
## Design reports|The right chart for each question, and highlighting key numbers.
Choosing the right visual|Choosing a visual depends on your question. For a trend over time use a line chart. To compare categories use bars. To show simple shares use a pie with at most five parts. For one important number use a card.^First ask yourself: what should the reader understand in three seconds?|line:Jan,Feb,Mar,Apr/20,35,30,50|Which visual best shows the sales trend across months?~Line chart~Pie chart~Card|0
Cards and KPIs|A Card shows one big number and is the best way to highlight key indicators such as total sales. Put it at the top of the page because the eye starts there. A KPI visual adds a comparison with a target and a trend.^Example: cards for sales, profit, and number of customers in the first row.|kpi:Sales,1.2M;Profit,300K;Customers,845|According to the cards, how many customers are there?~845~300K~1.2M|0
## Interaction and storytelling|Slicers, filters, and moving between levels.
Slicers and filters|A slicer is a filter the user can see and pick from, such as city or year, and every visual on the page changes. There is also the Filters pane, which lets you filter one visual, one page, or the whole report.^If you want the slicer choice to carry to other pages, turn on Sync slicers.|pie:Riyadh,Jeddah,Dammam/50,30,20|The chart is before filtering. What appears if you pick "Jeddah" in the slicer?~Jeddah data only~All cities~Nothing|0
Navigation: Drill and Drillthrough|When data is arranged in levels (year > quarter > month > day) you can move between them. Drill down goes to a finer level and Drill up goes to a broader one. Drill through takes you to a detail page about the item you picked.^This needs the visual to be built on a hierarchy.|flow:Year,Quarter,Month,Day|Going from "Year" to "Quarter" is called?~Drill down~Drill up~Sync slicers|0
# Analyze data in Power BI|Find trends and forecast, and use smart visuals.|perform-analytics-power-bi
## Trends and forecasting|Reading direction and estimating the future.
Trend line|Power BI analytics helps you understand direction. From the Analytics pane you add a trend line that shows whether values are rising or falling, plus an average line and a reference line.^Example: sales bounce around each week, but the trend line shows they are clearly rising.|line:Week 1,Week 2,Week 3,Week 4,Week 5,Week 6/10,14,13,18,21,25|What is the overall direction of sales in the chart?~Rising~Falling~Flat|0
Forecast|Forecast extends the time line into the future based on the pattern of the past, and shows a confidence band that tells you how uncertain the prediction is. It works on a line chart whose axis is a date.^Remember: a forecast is an estimate, not a guarantee, and it improves with more history.|line:Q1,Q2,Q3,Q4,Q5 fcst,Q6 fcst/20,24,28,31,35,38|What does Forecast rely on?~Past data~Random guessing~The color of the chart|0
## Smart visuals|Visuals that explain the numbers.
Decomposition tree and Key influencers|These are smart visuals that explain numbers. The Decomposition tree breaks a big number into its factors step by step. Key influencers analyzes which factors raise or lower a given result.^Example: why did sales fall? The tree shows that one region and one product are the cause.|flow:Total sales,By region,By product|The Decomposition tree breaks the number into?~Its factors, step by step~Pictures~Files|0
Q&A and Smart narrative|The Q&A visual lets you type a question in plain language such as "sales by city" and draws the right visual. Smart narrative writes a sentence that summarizes the chart and updates automatically when filters change.^Both help people who are not comfortable building visuals.|flow:You type a question,Power BI understands it,It draws a visual|What does the Q&A visual do?~Turns your text question into a visual~Deletes data~Prints the report|0
# Manage workspaces and models|Publishing, sharing, refreshing, and security in Power BI Service.|manage-workspaces-datasets-power-bi
## Workspaces and apps|Where the team works and how results are distributed.
Workspace and roles|A workspace is a place in Power BI Service where your team gathers around reports and models. Each person has a role: Admin manages everything, Member and Contributor edit content, and Viewer can only read.^Rule: give the least permission that lets the person do their job.|table:Role,Edits content,Read only/Contributor,Yes,No;Viewer,No,Yes|Who can edit content according to the table?~Contributor~Viewer~Both|0
Apps and sharing|When the reports are finished you publish them as an App for a wide audience. Users open the app and read without entering the workspace and without editing. You can update the app when the reports change.^Direct sharing (Share) suits one report and a few colleagues.|flow:Workspace,Publish an App,Users read|Why do we use an App?~To give many people read access without edit rights~To delete data~To create a database|0
## Refreshing models|Keeping data current.
Scheduled refresh and the gateway|Refreshing data keeps your report current. In Power BI Service you set a scheduled refresh: up to 8 times a day in Pro and 48 in Premium. If the source is inside your company network you need a gateway, which acts as a secure bridge.^Install the gateway on a machine that is always on.|flow:Source inside the company,Gateway,Power BI Service|What do you need to refresh a source inside the company network?~A gateway~Excel~A slicer|0
Incremental refresh|Incremental refresh helps with huge tables. It refreshes only the recent part, such as the last week, and leaves the old archive as it is, which saves time and resources.^Example: a 5-year sales table changes only in its last few days.|bar:Old archive,Last week/99,1|The chart shows the share of rows. Which part is reloaded?~The last week~The old archive~Both together|0
## Security and dashboards|Who sees what and how you follow the numbers.
Row-level security (RLS)|RLS, or row-level security, lets each user see only their own rows. You create a role with a filter such as [Region] = "Gulf" and then add people to it. It matters when several managers share one report.^Note: the rule applies to people whose role is Viewer, and does not apply to Admin, Member, or Contributor.|table:Region,Sales/Gulf,500;Europe,300|The RLS role is set to "Gulf". What does the manager see according to the table?~The Gulf row only~Both rows~Nothing|0
Dashboards and alerts|A dashboard is a single page in the Service that gathers visuals pinned from several reports. You can also add a data alert on a card or KPI that sends a notification when the number crosses a limit you set.^Alerts work on visuals pinned to a dashboard, not inside reports.|kpi:Sales today,95K;Minimum,100K|Is the alert condition "sales below 100K" met?~Yes because 95K is below 100K~No~Cannot tell|0`;
